alled solar panels at its plant for reduced electricity costs and energy independence. It is also considering manufacture of allied products in the textile sector.","src":"drhp","as_of":""},"moat":{"title":"Strengths (RHP)","body":"- integration: fully integrated Design > Fabrics > Garments business model with in-house fabric dyeing, rotary fabric printing and garment stitching — evidence: 15 tons/day fabric dyeing, 6 tons/day rotary fabric printing, and 10,000 pcs/day garment stitching & finishing capacity disclosed in the corporate overview.\n- cost position: lowest utility consumption due to modernized machinery reducing water, steam and power costs — evidence: company states 'Lowest water, steam and power consumption due to modernized machinery' in the at-a-glance section.\n- customer lock-in: integration and order-based …","src":"drhp","as_of":""},"risks":{"title":"Risks (RHP)","body":"- Price fluctuation on basic raw materials like cotton, yarn, chemicals, power and finished goods used in manufacturing.\n- Primary raw material is cotton and cotton yarn sourced from the domestic market; cotton is an agricultural product whose supply and quality are subject to forces of nature, and any shortage or deterioration could raise production costs that may not be passable to customers.\n- Slow growth in the global economy impacts demand and export realization for garments.\n- Any increase in interest rate can affect the company's finance cost.\n- Company exports to African and other European countries; volatility in currency markets can impact overall profitability.\n- Company's ability to deliver value depends on its ability to attract, retain and nurture talent; attrition and non-availability of required talent can affect overall performance.\n- Company is exposed to competition from Asian countries like Sri Lanka, China, Taiwan and other African countries, which can create pressure on margins and market share.\n- Exports are subject to set legal procedures and govern